you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  wildfires continue to rip through turkey  italy and greece  as forecasters say temperatures could  reach a record high in europe of 49  degrees this week how climate change is  threatening the availability and  quality of water around the world  and trying to green the construction  material responsible for more emissions  than aviation and deforestation  combined  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions  and we start today with wildfires that  are continuing to rage in turkey greece  and  italy in turkey whole villages and  beaches have been evacuated  with coastal areas amongst the worst  affected  flames have surrounded the tourist  resort of marmarus with residents  forced to flee as their homes were  destroyed  more than 100 firefighters are battling  to bring the fires  under control in the italian city of  pescara on the adriatic coast people  ran from the beach when they spotted  smoke and flames  rising from a nearby forest more than  100 people have been evacuated after  wildfires swept through the italian  coastal town  several people were injured when they  tried to extinguish flames that had  reached their homes  in greece wildfires have destroyed more  than 3 000 hectares of  pine and olive groves in the country's  west since saturday  the country's prime minister said that  greece is experiencing its  worst heat wave in more than 30 years  so what's behind this well temperatures  in this region would normally be around  the high 20s  to low 30s but in recent days we've seen  them hit  10 to 15 degrees above average and those  figures  are set to continue tomorrow and into  the rest of the week  well the heat is building because of a  blocking area of high pressure  in the upper atmosphere now the native  air mass  over southeast europe was already hot  and the prolonged spell of high pressure  has exacerbated the hot and dry  conditions it's all to do with  subsidence as high pressure causes the  air to sink which warms it  and then the heat builds in other words  it creates a heat  dome while high temperatures will  continue around the mediterranean and  balkan peninsula  this week maximum temperatures will  range from the high  30s to mid-40s and a few spots could  reach 47  to 49 degrees and this would challenge  the european record  of 48 degrees which was set in greece in  1997.  sky's special correspondent alice  crawford is in bodrum in turkey and has  witnessed the ferocity of the fires  first hand look at this fire that's just  a spark has just landed right next to us  um and that shows just how quickly it  can catch a hold we've just come from  down that road look at that road it's  now getting  overwhelmed by fire at the end of that  road  there's a small village of several  thousand people  um from churchetmi which is a near  bottom  and just within the space of the last 20  minutes or so  the fire has engulfed the whole of our  rear  um road and has come up along the side  and is threatening to overwhelm this  area with there was mass panic  in the village with dozens and dozens of  people  racing to get out of the area including  the firefighters  truckloads of firefighters pulling out  now we arrived there first thing this  morning  and these strong winds are part of the  reason why  it is um really gathering pace  let's move a bit further down kevin  because it's coming further behind us  and i don't want to get cut off  but um just keep going if you can  um and there was a lot of panic  in the village as we were there you can  see it it's coming down the sides of the  the hill there there was a lot of panic  in the village because they were pretty  much on their own  there wasn't uh firefighters uh we  arrived as they were actually taking  bottles of water and pulling out buckets  of  of water from their wells and trying to  douse the flames and protect their  property  temperatures are much higher than normal  they've been at  over 40 degrees centigrade and that is  the forecast for the next  week or so and many are putting this  down to  a real dramatic shift in climate change  turkey's always had seasonal dry periods  it's always had hot weather  this is far far hotter than anyone  anticipated  as i say the worst in at least a decade  and there'll be more on this developing  story as well as all the day's other  climate news on our website skynews.com  climate now which says people are put  off switching from a petrol to an  electric car  because of the high up-front costs the  consumer group analyzed the cost of  owning  three vehicles that are available as  petrol and electric versions  and found it was significantly more  expensive to own the electric version  over three years  the climate change committee says the  transition to electric cars is one of  the most important actions to achieve  the uk's  net zero target  farmers in england have been given an  extra 17 million pounds in funding to  tackle water and air pollution  the government says every farm will have  access to the programme by march  2023 an increase of 60 percent from its  current coverage  the scheme helps farmers to implement  practical solutions like planting  new grassland buffer strips and  establishing  riverside trees  plastic pollution is creating a trap for  young sea turtles who spend their early  years  stuck in some of the worst affected  waters research led by the university of  exeter  found the animals swallow plastic as  they travel on currents in the pacific  and indian oceans  scientists say it could have a  significant impact on population levels  with global efforts to reduce emissions  and limit global warming affecting  everything from transport to food  there's one area proving  harder to tackle construction and more  specifically  cement the active ingredient in concrete  is responsible for around  seven percent of global carbon emissions  but according to a senior environmental  expert  far more needs to be done to regulate  the industry as our economics and data  editor ed conway reports  it is quite literally the foundational  material of modern civilization  concrete in foundations for power  stations like this  in bridges roads buildings and railways  like hs2  it is the very skeleton of britain's  physical economy  so much so that we sometimes take it for  granted but concrete and its crucial  ingredients cement  are now at the heart of a very big issue  indeed  now when you think about net zero what  probably comes to mind cars  planes power stations but arguably  what's happening in  cement plants like this in this country  and around the world is just as if not  more important  for the future of the planet what we do  here is we take chalk and clay and we  introduce it to this rotary kiln that  you can see behind you  where it's heated to 1 500 degrees  centigrade  and converted into cement that matters  because cement production accounts for  about seven percent of global co2  emissions  that's even bigger than all aviation and  deforestation combined  and here's the key thing cement  emissions come from two sources  first the energy used to heat the kilns  but even more so the chemical  reaction going on inside that rotating  tube  this plant uses renewable fuels  essentially processed rubbish for the  energy bit  but that still leaves the chemical  reaction one option is to try to capture  those emissions and put them back  underground  but that won't happen without  intervention you can't expect companies  to do this out of the goodness of their  hearts  so it's got to be regulated and it's got  to be fair  so you know ideally all we should set a  timetable for decarbonizing cement  across the whole of europe every system  can be mixed roughly the same amount of  time  but mighty answer lie here in these test  tubes at the university of sheffield  that's the zero co2 system that's right  that cement in the test tube on the  right was made without any of the direct  carbon emissions caused by traditional  cement manufacturer  can we just replace traditional cement  with this stuff the short answer is no  and that's because one of the biggest  challenges that we have with these  materials with any replacement for  portland cement  is to be able to produce it on a scale  that can meet the demand for cement and  concrete  all of which is why concrete boring  lifeless concrete  is fast becoming the most exciting  dynamic field in climate science  unless we solve these problems net zero  will remain  a pipe dream ed conway sky news  now climate change will increasingly  affect water availability and quality  around the world with devastating  consequences for the most vulnerable  that's according to a new report from  the university of oxford's reach  programme  which found women are disproportionately  affected by extreme weather such as  floods  while heavy rainfall can lead to  outbreaks of disease  as waste is washed into water systems  now the report looked at how risks vary  in different areas for example in cities  the availability and quality of water  can be put at risk from industrial  pollution  and urbanization now to improve climate  resilience  large areas require better coordination  of surface and groundwater supply  and demand management schemes as smaller  urban areas grow  better communication of climate  information will be vital  local forecasts can help avoid water  shortages  reducing inequalities from unreliable  supplies  while those living in rural areas are  highly vulnerable to climate shocks  and the risks aren't shared equally  impacting women more than men  when climate change affects supplies  people have to adapt their livelihoods  without sufficient information on  emerging risks  well dr katrina charles is associate  professor at the university of oxford  and co-director of reach with water  quality  we know with heavy rains it's going to  flush more  animal waste and human waste into the  waterways into water systems so drinking  water irrigation systems  and so on and these are going to cause  more diseases such as  typhoid and cholera on the other hand  when you've got more dry weather  you find that pollution that's going in  the river like industrial pollution  and that that becomes more concentrated  in the river so people are more exposed  to some of the toxic chemicals like  chromium  well let's bring in professor daniel  alargo from the university of nairobi  and principal investigator for reach  in kenya welcome to you tell us about  some of the challenges that you're  facing in kenya when it comes to water  security  among some of the problems that we  experience are too much rain  too little rain very clean water  then very dirty water and  sometimes the water is too far away to  be able to get it  within a reasonable amount of time  there are also other experiences that  people have in relation to the water  issue and that is in terms of uh you  know  agriculture in terms of uh  you know what do we plant during this  season  and what do we expect to reap at the end  of the day  so it ties in a lot also with aspects of  food  security and it also ties in with  aspects of health and in particular  it has been noted in urban areas more  than in rural areas  that the lack of integration of gender  issues into wash programs  is actually disadvantaging women  disproportionately to men  when it comes to accessing the water  resource so these are just some of the  problems that we do experience  so what can you do about them how can  you improve the situation  uh the first thing is we need to  understand our climate system  and that is one of the things that we  ventured to do in this rich project  to get this information on a much more  granular level  to reduce uncertainties and to enable  better quantification  for scenario analysis and also trade-off  analysis  as well as preparing for present and  future risks  professor daniel largo thanks very much  indeed thank you  well that's everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show find out why  countries need to rely  on more than just planting trees to  achieve net zero targets  thanks for watching see you then

